module bindings.go.presenceDerivedEmailVerification;

// OpenID Connect Core 5.1 defines `email_verified` as a claim the provider asserts
// -- "True if the End-User's e-mail address has been verified; otherwise false" --
// and 5.7 says a relying party must not key anything off `email` alone, because the
// address is mutable and reassignable. Every provider that returns an address at all
// documents its own attestation beside it: GitHub's and Discord's user endpoints ship
// a `verified` boolean, GitLab's /user a `confirmed_at` timestamp that stays null
// until the confirmation link is followed, Bitbucket's /user/emails an `is_confirmed`
// flag per address, LinkedIn's and Microsoft Entra ID's userinfo the OIDC claim
// itself. An adapter that instead writes `EmailVerified: profile.Email != ""` has
// recorded that the endpoint returned a string, which every one of them does for an
// unconfirmed address too -- so an account whose address was typed in and never
// confirmed arrives at the relying party looking verified (CWE-287/CWE-290). Where
// that flag decides whether a federated identity may be attached to a local account
// matching the same address, it is an account takeover.
//
// Three facts are that shape. A struct field named for email verification being set
// in the body; a `!= ""` comparison whose left side is email-named, which is the
// presence test standing in for the attestation; and no read of any verification
// field the provider actually supplies.
//
// That last clause is the neutraliser and is deliberately generous. It names the
// documented claim spellings -- `EmailVerified`, `Verified`, `ConfirmedAt`,
// `IsConfirmed` and their variants -- so an adapter that reads the provider's signal
// is not reported however it then combines it, including GitLab's documented form
// where the confirmation timestamp is conjoined with the presence test
// (`Email != "" && ConfirmedAt != ""`). It also means an adapter that reads the
// attestation and then falls back past it is not reported; that is the recall cost of
// keeping the common correct form quiet, and the fallback shape is better described
// by a control-flow fact than by a presence one.
//
// The email predicate is listed in both capitalisations because the engine matches
// binary-expression operands case-sensitively, and Go spells the address either
// `email` on a local or `Email` on a struct field.
binding goPresenceDerivedEmailVerification {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "go" and containsAny(node.context.field, ["EmailVerified", "email_verified", "EmailIsVerified", "IsEmailVerified", "EmailConfirmed", "email_confirmed", "VerifiedEmail", "verified_email"]) and containsAny(node.context.binary, ["Email!=\"\"", "email!=\"\""]) and not containsAny(node.context.selector, [".EmailVerified", ".IsEmailVerified", ".EmailIsVerified", ".VerifiedEmail", ".EmailConfirmed", ".EmailVerifiedAt", ".Verified", ".IsVerified", ".ConfirmedAt", ".IsConfirmed", ".ConfirmedEmail"])
  emit issue code.PresenceDerivedEmailVerification at node
}
